{"answer":"The critic's case does not succeed against the target as specified. The critic equates 'finding a maximal sub-relation satisfying a monotonicity or stance property' with 'finding a maximal admissible set' and declares NP-completeness, but this equivalence is not established by the critic and is not warranted by the target. The target does not require computing an arbitrary maximal admissible set of an argumentation framework. It specifies a finite family of monotone fixed-point operators and a single monotone pass over the operator lattice to select the operator whose accepted set is the maximal sub-relation satisfying the school's stance. A monotone fixed-point operator on a finite lattice reaches its least fixed point by polynomially many lattice-ascending iterations in the lattice height; the selection among a finite family of such operators, under a committed school input, is a finite bounded choice, not a combinatorial search over all admissible sets. The critic's NP-completeness charge would apply to computing maximal admissible sets of an arbitrary argumentation framework under admissibility semantics, but the target replaces that search with an explicit, committed selector over monotone operators. The critic does not establish that the operator lattice here has exponential height or that the selector enumerates an exponential candidate space; absent that, no worst-case exponential lower bound follows. The critic's second clause, that 'maximal sub-relation satisfying a stance' is not well-defined without a canonical ordering over relations, also does not establish a defect: maximality in a finite lattice is a lattice-theoretic notion independent of any external ordering over relations; a greatest or maximal element satisfying a property is defined by the partial order of the lattice, not by a canonical total order. The target's determinism is preserved because the school is committed as first-class input, and the selector is evaluated per committed school over the finite monotone operator family. The critic at most raises an abstract worry that hardness could be displaced rather than removed, but displacement would require showing that the selector or the operator-lattice pass hides an equivalent NP-hard subproblem; the critic provides no such reduction. Nothing in the target's specification entails that the operator-lattice pass or the finite selection is worst-case non-polynomial, and the critic has not established otherwise. The polynomial cost guarantee is therefore not shown to be violated."}